    <h2>The gap most test prep never trains</h2>
    <p>Maybe your teen does well on practice tests, then falls apart on the real one. Or they understand the math at the kitchen table and blank on it once the timer starts.</p>
    <p>That's not a knowledge gap. It's the one thing most test prep doesn't train &mdash; what happens to their thinking once the pressure's real.</p>
    <p>I'm Joe Ruotolo. I've worked with a lot of students, and I've seen months of preparation come undone because a student walked into the test dysregulated, with no tools to get their head back in the game.</p>
    <p>I built Third Mind Learning to solve that problem, so students walk in with both the knowledge and the state-management skills they need to perform at their best.</p>
    <p>That anxious, locked-up feeling isn't who they are.</p>
    <p>It's a state. And with the right tools, states can change.</p>
    <figure class="quote">
      <blockquote>
        <p>&ldquo;The heart coherence work made a real difference. It improved my focus and helped me manage the stress and anxiety I used to feel about testing.&rdquo;</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>&mdash; Mason G, SAT Student</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
    <h2>Test prep for the material. State management for the pressure.</h2>
    <p>The tools come from two sides.</p>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>Test-prep strategy covers the material: content mastery, error analysis, pacing. Every student starts with a diagnostic test. What it shows decides what they practice, so their time goes to the areas that need it most.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>State management covers the pressure. Built on HeartMath&reg; heart-coherence training, it teaches students to recognize an anxious state and shift out of it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Most students get taught one side or the other. Rarely both, together, on purpose.</p>
    <figure class="quote">
      <blockquote>
        <p>&ldquo;Your approach taught me how to actually do the math and understand why it works, instead of memorizing steps to plug into a calculator.&rdquo;</p>
      </blockquote>
      <figcaption>&mdash; SAT Student</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</div>

